## JavaScript

### Formats

- Use `const` by default, `let` only for reassignment, never `var`.
- Compare with `===`/`!==`; use `??`/`?.` where `0` and `""` are valid values.
- Always brace control-flow bodies (`if`/`else`, `for`, `while`, `do`) even for a single statement.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Formats -->

### Names

- Use `camelCase` for values and functions, `PascalCase` for classes and components, `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` only for constants shared across modules.
- Name a file after the one thing it exports, in the project's casing convention — never two conventions in one folder.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Names -->

### Packages

- Write ES modules (`import`/`export`); never mix `require` into new code.
- Prefer named exports; use a default export only where a framework demands one.
- Export only what another module uses; everything else stays module-private.
- Avoid barrel files re-exporting whole folders — they hide cycles and load unwanted code; import from the owning module.
- Never keep mutable state at module level; pass collaborators in.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Packages -->

### Types

- Describe cross-module data shapes with JSDoc `@typedef`, or move the module to TypeScript.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Types -->

### Functions

- Never mutate arguments; prefer `map`, `filter`, spread, `toSorted`, `toSpliced`, `with`.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Functions -->

### Comments

<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Comments -->

### Errors

- Throw `Error` subclasses, never strings or plain objects; chain the cause: `throw new PaymentError("charge failed", { cause: error })`.
- Never leave a promise floating: await it, return it, or hand it to a handler; never write an empty `catch`.
- Cancel with `AbortController` and pass its `signal` through every call that can wait.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Errors -->

### Concurrency

- Start independent operations with `Promise.all` or `Promise.allSettled`, not one `await` at a time in a loop.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Concurrency -->

### Tests

- Use the project's test runner; keep tests deterministic: fake timers or an injected clock, no real network.
- Name tests for behavior (`rejects an empty cart`) and assert outcomes, never internals.
- Await every asynchronous assertion; a missing `await` makes a test pass forever.
- Test boundary values; add tests around recent bugs.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Tests -->

### Smells

- Callback pyramids and `.then` chains that drop rejections.
- Truthiness bugs with `0`, `""`, and `NaN`.
- Functions that mutate their arguments.
- A `utils.js` collecting unrelated helpers.
- Barrels that re-export everything and hide cycles.
- Built-in prototypes extended by the application.
- `console.log` left on production paths.
<!-- Project-specific / JavaScript-Smells -->
